    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_402b2106a22bfa8db6cf863c567136f07deec01aa91a7156bb995808fe4f475f",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source fact: the authors extend a prior rigorous error analysis to the larger height range and account for the CYBER 205's truncated 47- and 94-bit arithmetic. Interpolation dominates the error of method A, while truncation of the two-term Riemann-Siegel expansion dominates method B; other hardware terms are smaller. The fixed bounds actually used are deliberately larger than the derived safe bounds, and method B determines every needed Gram-point sign. Interpretation: an approximation plus an absolute-error envelope is the interface from floating-point output to a certified exact sign. Boundary: the detailed derivation is referred to earlier work, and the stated bounds apply to these methods, machines, and range.",
